# IntellyRouter runtime environment.
#
# Copy to .env and edit, then pass `--env-file .env` to docker compose or
# use the Variables section on Dokploy / Coolify.
#
# A running gateway needs three things: an address to bind, a writable
# data directory, and an eval tasks directory. Everything else is optional.

# Where the gateway listens. localhost by default. Dokploy / Coolify must
# publish the container port (7117) and front it with TLS; the gateway
# itself does not terminate TLS.
INTELLYROUTER_ADDR=0.0.0.0:7117

# Data directory. Mapped to a persistent volume under Dokploy /
# Coolify so master.key survives restarts; see docker-compose.yml.
INTELLYROUTER_DATA=/data

# Eval task directory. Eval tasks ship with the gateway; override this
# when you want to add or edit tasks without rebuilding the image.
INTELLYROUTER_EVAL_TASKS=/eval-tasks

# IMPORTANT — what does NOT work in a container:
#
# The gateway relays subscription traffic end-to-end, so subscription
# routes work in a container. What does not work is the parts of the
# gateway that originate their own subscription calls, because the
# container has no Claude Code to log in with. The following need
# `claude` on PATH inside the container:
#
#  - The Claude-Code director (`DirectorSettings.ClaudeCode: true`)
#    shells out to `claude`. Install Claude Code in the image or use
#    an API-key director instead.
#  - The subscription advisor uses the same `claude` call.
#  - The eval runner (`intelly-eval`) spawns `claude -p` against a
#    logged-in session. Run it on the host.
#
# Everything else (subscription passthrough, API-key directors, advisor
# on API-key models, classifier, escalation tiers, combos) is fine
# inside a container.
#
# Configure API-key providers in the dashboard after first boot, then
# set the routes and tiers you want from the Providers / Routes / Combos
# pages.
